🐍 Where Do Types Come From in Python?
 
    Python, like every other programming language, has a core set of fundamental types that serve as the basic building blocks for all programs. 
    These are often referred to as the Built-in Types and are part of the Python Standard Library.

    They provide the essential capabilities required to perform core programming tasks.

    You have spent a lot of time with str (String) and int (Integer) types, but there are also many more.

Python's Core Built-in Types

    Here are some of the other types from the Python standard library that you will encounter:
    list, dict, set, tuple, float, bool


🔡 Python Naming Conventions (Type vs. Variable)
    In Python, the naming conventions for types and variables are also intentionally different to make the code easier to read, though the exact rules differ from Swift.

    Element: Built-in Types			

        Python Convention: Lowercase

        Example: int, str, list, bool

        Explanation: Built-in types are generally all lowercase.


    Element: Classes (Custom Types)

        Python Convention: Upper Camel Case (PascalCase)

        Example: class MyClass:
	
        Explanation: Custom-defined types (classes) start with a capital letter and follow Upper Camel Case.


    Element: Variables/Functions

        Python Convention: Lowercase with Underscores (snake_case)

        Example: my_variable, calculate_sum()
        
        Explanation: Standard variables and functions use lowercase letters and separate words with underscores.


    The difference is intentional:

        Custom Types (Classes) start with a capital letter (e.g., Car, Employee).
        Variables, Functions, and Built-in Types all start with a lowercase letter (e.g., age, get_name, int).


    This convention makes it easy to visually distinguish a custom type (something you can create an instance of) from a regular variable, function, or one of Python's fundamental built-in types.